      "text": "To determine the number that is a multiple of every integer, we need to understand the definition of a multiple. A number \\( x \\) is a multiple of another number \\( y \\) if there exists an integer \\( k \\) such that \\( x = ky \\).\n\nLet's denote this common multiple by \\( N \\). According to the problem, \\( N \\) must be a multiple of every integer. This means that for any integer \\( n \\), there must exist an integer \\( k_n \\) such that \\( N = n k_n \\).\n\nTo find such an \\( N \\), we need to consider the product of the smallest positive integers because any integer can be expressed as a product of these smallest integers. The smallest positive integers are 1, 2, 3, etc. The product of these smallest integers is:\n\n\\[\nN = 1 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times \\cdots = \\infty\n\\]\n\nHowever, this is not the correct approach. Instead, we should consider the least common multiple (LCM) of these smallest integers. The LCM of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the integers in the set.\n\nFor example:\n- The LCM of 1, 2 is 2.\n- The LCM of 1, 2, 3 is 6.\n- The LCM of 1, 2, 3, 4 is 12.\n- And so on.\n\nThe LCM of the first \\( n \\) positive integers is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n) = \\frac{1 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times \\cdots \\times n}{\\text{GCD}(1, 2, 3, \\ldots, n)}\n\\]\n\nAs \\( n \\) approaches infinity, the LCM becomes infinite. However, the key insight is that the LCM of the smallest set of integers (1, 2, 3, etc.) is theoretically infinite, but in practical terms, it is a very large number. The only number that can consistently be a multiple of every integer (in terms of being divisible by each integer without remainder) is the number 0, because:\n\n- Any integer \\( n \\) can be written as \\( n = n \\cdot 1 \\), and 0 is a multiple of every integer (since \\( 0 \\cdot k = 0 \\) for any integer \\( k \\)).\n\nTherefore, the number that is a multiple of every integer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
