      "text": "To find the number that is a multiple of every integer, let's denote this number by \\( n \\).\n\nFor \\( n \\) to be a multiple of every integer \\( k \\), \\( n \\) must satisfy the condition:\n\\[ n \\text{ is divisible by } k \\]\nfor every positive integer \\( k \\).\n\nThis means that for any positive integer \\( k \\),\n\\[ n = k \\cdot m \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nLet's consider a few small values of \\( k \\):\n1. When \\( k = 1 \\), \\( n = 1 \\cdot m = m \\), which is true for some integer \\( m \\).\n2. When \\( k = 2 \\), \\( n = 2 \\cdot m = 2m \\), which is true for some integer \\( m \\).\n3. When \\( k = 3 \\), \\( n = 3 \\cdot m = 3m \\), which is true for some integer \\( m \\).\n\nSo, \\( n \\) must be a multiple of 1, 2, and 3. However, for \\( n \\) to be a multiple of every integer, it must be a multiple of every positive integer. Let's consider the prime factorization of the positive integers:\n- \\( 1 = 1 \\)\n- \\( 2 = 2 \\)\n- \\( 3 = 3 \\)\n- \\( 4 = 2^2 \\)\n- \\( 5 = 5 \\)\n- \\( 6 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\)\n- \\( 7 = 7 \\)\n- \\( 8 = 2^3 \\)\n- \\( 9 = 3^2 \\)\n- \\( 10 = 2 \\cdot 5 \\)\n- and so on...\n\nFor \\( n \\) to be a multiple of every integer, \\( n \\) must include all prime factors with at least the same multiplicity. The smallest number that satisfies this condition is the least common multiple (LCM) of all positive integers.\n\nThe LCM of all positive integers is infinite since there are infinitely many prime numbers. However, we can also consider the product of all positive integers. The product of all positive integers is denoted by \\( \\prod_{k=1}^{\\infty} k \\), which is the factorial of infinity:\n\\[ \\infty! = \\infty \\cdot (\\infty-1) \\cdot (\\infty-2) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 \\]\n\nHowever, this product is not a finite number. Instead, we need to consider the smallest number that is a multiple of every integer, which is \\( 2^a \\cdot 3^b \\cdots p^n \\) where \\( p \\) is any prime number, and \\( a, b, \\ldots, n \\) are all sufficiently large integers. The smallest such number is \\( 2^1 \\cdot 3^1 \\cdot 5^1 \\cdot 7^1 \\cdots \\), which is \\( 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\cdots \\), but this is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the only number that satisfies the condition is the number that is a multiple of every integer, which is infinity in a generalized sense. However, the only finite number that can be considered to be a multiple of every integer in a practical sense is \\( 12 \\), because it is the least common multiple of the first five positive integers (1, 2, 3, 4, 5), and it is the smallest number that is a multiple of all these integers.\n\nThus, the number that is a multiple of every integer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
